  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual framework for improving self-driving laboratory (SDL) safety with AI. At the center is a circular workflow linking two complementary sides: automated laboratory hardware, shown as a robotic arm with a lock symbol, and human oversight supported by AI, shown as a scientist beside a computer labeled “AI.” The loop suggests an ongoing interaction between these elements when dealing with “challenging chemistries” and “high-risk experiments.”\n\nOn the left, the image highlights ways AI can strengthen SDL safety directly within the experimental platform. These include cognitive safety, vision-based safety, more robust chemical handling and motion planning, addressing the “curse of rarity,” and creating digital safety twins. Together, these points imply AI helps both perceive hazards and make safer decisions in automated experimentation.\n\nOn the right, the figure broadens the message to a larger “robust SDL safety ecosystem.” This includes workforce training, SDL safety level classification, collaboration between stakeholders, and instilling trust. Overall, the figure argues that safe autonomous chemistry depends not only on smarter robotic systems and AI tools, but also on human-centered governance, training, and shared safety standards.",
